You are writing unit tests for the `mlt` MATLAB linter (Rust workspace at /home/watermarkhu/repositories/mlt). Add a comprehensive `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block to TWO files in `crates/mlt_rules/src/`:
1. `naming.rs` — the `NamingEngine` (file-level rule; 81 rule IDs `naming.<entity>.<checkType>`; only maxLength/minLength fire with default config — maxLength default 63, minLength default 2)
2. `custom_checks.rs` — the `CustomChecksEngine` (file-level metrics engine, 25 check IDs, configurable thresholds)

READ FIRST, in this order:
- `crates/mlt_rules/src/test_util.rs` — the shared test helpers you MUST use (parse, file_ctx, lint_nodes, lint_file, has_id).
- `crates/mlt_rules/src/formatting.rs` lines ~810 to end — the canonical example test module to imitate.
- Read both target files FULLY. For naming.rs, understand the 81 rule IDs, how entities are extracted (class/function/variable/etc.), and which checks fire with default config. For custom_checks.rs, read all 25 check IDs, their thresholds, and the config struct fields (e.g. `max_line_length`, `max_function_length`, etc.).

APPEND to each file (at the very end, after the `inventory::submit!` block) a test module like this:

```rust
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tests
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use crate::test_util::{has_id, lint_file, lint_nodes, parse};
    use mlt_core::Config;

    fn engine() -> Box<dyn Rule> {
        <EngineName>::from_config(&Config::default())
    }
    // ...
}
```

GUIDELINES:
- naming.rs (use `lint_file`): with default config, a variable/function name shorter than 2 chars should fire `naming.variable.minLength` (or `naming.function.minLength`); a long name (> 63 chars) should fire maxLength; normal names should not. ALSO test config-respected behavior: build a `Config::from_toml` that sets naming rules (read the module to find how the config struct works, e.g. `NamingConfig`), enabling e.g. `requiredPrefix` or `casing`, and verify it fires. Cover at least: minLength fires, maxLength fires, minLength does not fire on a 2+ char name, and at least one config-enabled check (e.g. prefix or casing) fires when configured.
- custom_checks.rs (use `lint_file`): cover representative checks. With default thresholds, e.g. cyclomatic complexity (check the default threshold and craft a function exceeding it) and line-length checks. Prefer crafting code that exceeds defaults, AND/OR use `Config::from_toml` to lower thresholds to make checks fire on small inputs (read the config struct field names first). Write positive + negative tests for at least ~10 of the 25 check IDs.
- Test inputs are MATLAB source strings. You MUST verify each input actually triggers the intended check by RUNNING: `cargo test -p mlt_rules naming`, `cargo test -p mlt_rules custom_checks`. Use `-- --nocapture` to see diagnostics.
- If a check won't fire no matter the input, skip it and note why. Do NOT leave failing tests.
- If an input produces unexpected results, add a TEMPORARY tree-dump test to understand the parse structure, then DELETE it before finishing.
- Do NOT modify anything outside your two assigned files. Do NOT touch lib.rs, test_util.rs, other modules, or data files.

VERIFY before finishing (must all pass):
- `cargo build`
- `cargo test -p mlt_rules naming` — all pass
- `cargo test -p mlt_rules custom_checks` — all pass
- `cargo clippy --all-targets 2>&1 | grep -A3 'warning'` — ZERO warnings introduced by your files (ignore unrelated warnings elsewhere).

Report back: the tests added per module (count), check IDs covered, any skips and why, and confirmation that cargo test + clippy pass.

